@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:verification;security] fleet-native-trust-evidence
Native trust acceptance MUST inspect both actual guest distributions and their
current host roots, durable boot receipts and trust/network/SSH ordering. A
wrong-boot request carrying a different valid root set MUST complete with peer
EOF and no response bytes before inspecting installed trust; sender timeout MUST
fail rather than count as refusal. Wrong, forged and expired delivery mutations
(a legacy protocol version, a foreign placement, an expired execution epoch, a
malformed nonce, a forged digest claim and forged certificate material, including
a duplicate root and private-key-bearing PEM) MUST each terminate in the same
completed refusal before the receipt-unchanged check, with the durable receipt,
installed guest bundle and guest services untouched. Successful current-boot
deliveries and confinement checks MUST bound the exchange; exact guest trust and
retained data MUST be checked before the last positive exchange, which the
receiver MUST still accept after the refusal sweep. No fixture may write a
successful host receipt or replace observed physical capacity. Guest terminal
bytes and boot challenges MUST remain private even when the check fails.
Failure diagnostics MUST use a closed stage vocabulary without exception values
or command output. Installed trust MUST be inspected using tools actually
present in both immutable guest images; this is not a live TLS handshake claim.
Every shell input including completion markers MUST fit within 3072 bytes;
bundle reconstruction MUST append bounded pieces and compare the complete bytes
before any positive delivery can repair changed trust.
The existing bounded service-readiness observation MUST require the actual SSH
ready marker and a successful roster sync together with active required units;
periodic marker replacement MUST NOT be mistaken for persistent unavailability.
The fixture MUST NOT create markers or restart services to satisfy this check.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:verification;capacity] fleet-native-physical-refusal
Three usable NixOS guests MUST retain exactly three disk ownership holds, each
the fixture's manifest-derived NixOS root plus the 1 GiB workload bundle.
Settled virtual promises MUST NOT be treated as a physical admission cap.
The refusal control MUST keep the successful actor, image, profile and compute
shape, request a valid root larger than the independently read actual pool quota,
and observe insufficient_capacity without adding a VM or changing those holds.
The fixture MUST NOT derive this expectation from production Budget arithmetic.
